1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,280
Doc comments are the one description of a Swift package that sits right beside the code.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,140
And most of the time, nobody reads them.

3
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:09,630
They drift.

4
00:00:09,930 --> 00:00:12,750
They have no shape, so each one is different.

5
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:15,030
A tool can't rely on them.

6
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,980
gloss starts from a simple bet.

7
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:21,980
If every doc comment has the same shape, the docs can be checked,

8
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:24,500
and then they can be read as the interface they describe.

9
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,280
The checking is done by Swift Lucid, the linter gloss builds on.

10
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,260
It is its own project now.

11
00:00:33,010 --> 00:00:38,470
It checks every triple-slash comment against the house style a project sets in a file named

12
00:00:38,470 --> 00:00:40,410
dot swiftlucid dot json.

13
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:43,500
gloss's own docs keep one shape.

14
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:46,840
A summary of one or two sentences: what, then why.

15
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:48,000
A blank line.

16
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:52,400
Then a markup block, with an entry for every parameter, the return value,

17
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:53,380
and what it throws.

18
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:57,290
gloss shows those findings beside each declaration.

19
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:04,573
gloss reads every file once, through Swift Lucid, which parses it with swift-syntax.

20
00:01:05,243 --> 00:01:10,823
From that one pass, it builds each declaration's contract, and a graph of the dependencies between

21
00:01:10,823 --> 00:01:11,243
types.

22
00:01:11,993 --> 00:01:16,333
From those come an interface export, as JSON or a web page.

23
00:01:16,473 --> 00:01:21,113
A map of the package: its modules, folders, and types, and what each uses.

24
00:01:21,413 --> 00:01:26,273
The reason one type depends on another, in the words of the docs gloss already requires.

25
00:01:26,513 --> 00:01:31,913
What changed against a base revision, read straight from git, and what that change reaches.

26
00:01:33,933 --> 00:01:35,733
A branch can explain itself.

27
00:01:36,013 --> 00:01:39,753
It keeps a change story in a file under dot gloss, changes,

28
00:01:40,013 --> 00:01:41,033
named for the branch.

29
00:01:41,783 --> 00:01:44,863
A story has a headline, themes, and notes.

30
00:01:45,383 --> 00:01:47,103
Themes link the types they are about.

31
00:01:47,403 --> 00:01:49,703
A note tells the change from one type's side.

32
00:01:50,453 --> 00:01:52,613
Then gloss story checks it.

33
00:01:52,913 --> 00:01:57,153
It fails while a type whose interface changed has no theme, or no note,

34
00:01:57,333 --> 00:01:59,313
or while a link names nothing.

35
00:01:59,753 --> 00:02:01,833
A change can't land unexplained.

36
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:07,840
The package is built in layers, and each depends only on the ones below it.

37
00:02:08,590 --> 00:02:11,910
Lucid Read, from Swift Lucid, reads the sources.

38
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:16,090
Lucid Lint, also from Swift Lucid, holds the house style.

39
00:02:16,830 --> 00:02:18,690
Gloss Model holds what the docs say.

40
00:02:19,210 --> 00:02:21,450
Gloss Map arranges the model for reading.

41
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:26,290
Gloss Page draws it as a web page, and Gloss App draws it in a macOS

42
00:02:26,290 --> 00:02:26,610
window.

43
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:33,140
Gloss Model never imports Lucid Lint, so the model holds what the docs say whether or

44
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:34,100
not the lint runs.

45
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:38,790
The page and the app draw from the same map, so they can't disagree.

46
00:02:39,270 --> 00:02:43,430
One shape for the docs, one reading of the code, and a package you read through

47
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:44,170
its own words.
